  "reasoning": "The claim that \"lightning never strikes the same place twice\" is a common myth and scientifically false. Lightning naturally follows the path of least electrical resistance to ground, meaning tall, isolated, and conductive structures are struck repeatedly. For instance, according to meteorological data from NOAA and the National Weather Service, tall buildings like the Empire State Building are struck by lightning dozens of times every year, and individual lightning flashes can also strike the ground in multiple places or re-strike the same channel during a single event.",
